   "reviewNotes": [
    "Compound-angle and double-angle formulae are treated in the Upper Sixth pack; check whether the syllabus puts them in Lower Sixth.",
    "Syllabus references, scope and level of difficulty are to be checked against the official text; all facts, formulas and worked examples to be verified by a teacher before release."
   ],
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "propriete",
     "title": "Identities",
     "md": "$\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1$ and $\\tan x=\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$. Also $\\sin(180\\degree-x)=\\sin x$, $\\cos(360\\degree-x)=\\cos x$, $\\tan(180\\degree+x)=\\tan x$. Hence $1+\\tan^2x=\\frac{1}{\\cos^2x}$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "methode",
     "title": "Solving equations",
     "md": "1. Reduce to one trig function (use the identities).\n2. Find the principal value with the inverse function.\n3. Use symmetry (CAST / the graph) to find all other solutions in the interval.\n4. For $\\sin kx$, first solve for $kx$ over the enlarged interval, then divide by $k$."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "methode",
     "title": "Proving identities",
     "md": "Start from the more complicated side, replace $\\tan$ by $\\frac{\\sin}{\\cos}$, use $\\sin^2+\\cos^2=1$, and simplify until you reach the other side. Do not work on both sides at once."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "Never divide both sides by $\\sin x$ (you lose the solutions where $\\sin x=0$): factorise instead. A calculator gives only ONE solution; the interval usually contains more. For $\\sin2x=0.5$ in $0\\leq x\\leq180\\degree$ look for $2x$ up to $360\\degree$."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 1",
     "statement": "Solve $2\\sin x=1$ for $0\\degree\\leq x\\leq360\\degree$.",
     "steps": [
      "$\\sin x=\\frac{1}{2}$, principal value $x=30\\degree$.",
      "Sine is positive in quadrants 1 and 2: the second solution is $180\\degree-30\\degree=150\\degree$."
     ],
     "answer": "$x=30\\degree$ or $150\\degree$",
     "figure": {
      "kind": "plot",
      "xmin": 0,
      "xmax": 6.4,
